# Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# Exactly what has to be set before anything starts — everything else in this
# file is optional and documented where it appears:
#
#   DATABASE_URL      API refuses to boot without it, in every environment.
#   JWT_SECRET        Same. Minimum 32 characters.
#   SUPER_ADMIN_KEY   Same. Minimum 32 characters.
#   ENCRYPTION_KEY    Optional in development; the API refuses to boot WITHOUT
#                     it when NODE_ENV=production, and also refuses to boot on
#                     the value docker-compose.yml shipped as a default before
#                     2.0.0-rc.1. Generate your own: openssl rand -hex 32.
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD Required by docker-compose.yml (not by the API) — the
#   REDIS_PASSWORD    containers refuse to start without them. You must then
#                     paste both into DATABASE_URL / REDIS_URL by hand; compose
#                     does not substitute them for you.
#
# Redis has a working localhost default here, but it is not optional
# infrastructure: outbound webhook delivery runs on a Redis-backed queue and
# the API exits at boot if Redis is unreachable.

NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3030
HOST=0.0.0.0

# Datastore credentials. docker-compose.yml REQUIRES both — it refuses to start
# rather than fall back to a well-known default, since the previous default
# (`rekey`) shipped the same password on every deployment.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# Postgres + Redis connection strings. These are read by the API directly, so
# the passwords have to be spelled out here as well — replace BOTH occurrences
# of CHANGE_ME with the exact values you set above. compose does NOT substitute
# them for you.
#
# Running the test suite against dockerised Postgres? Set TEST_DATABASE_URL too
# (the default in test/global-setup.ts still assumes an unauthenticated local
# Postgres, which is no longer what compose gives you).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://rekey:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/rekey?schema=public

# Prisma connection-pool size per API process, and how long a query waits for a
# free connection before giving up. Applied to DATABASE_URL as
# `connection_limit` / `pool_timeout`; a value already in the URL wins.
#
# The default (20) is not Prisma's — Prisma's is `num_cpus * 2 + 1`, which on a
# 2-vCPU container is 5 connections shared between every HTTP handler and a
# webhook worker that runs 10 jobs concurrently. Size this above your worker
# concurrency, and keep (replicas x DATABASE_POOL_SIZE) under your Postgres
# `max_connections`.
# DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=20
# DATABASE_POOL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# Note the leading colon — Redis AUTH with requirepass has no username.
REDIS_URL=redis://:CHANGE_ME@localhost:6379

# How long (ms) one API process reuses an Application's organization-role
# catalog. A backstop only: changes propagate over Redis immediately. 0 disables
# the cache. Default 5000, max 300000. See docs/organization-roles.md.
# ORG_ROLE_CACHE_TTL_MS=5000

# How long (ms) one API process may reuse an operator's session, membership and
# grants. A backstop only: every revoke, sign-out, password change and role,
# scope or grant edit invalidates it at once, here and over Redis. 0 disables
# the cache. Default 5000, max 60000. See DEPLOY.md, "Database load".
# OPERATOR_AUTH_CACHE_TTL_MS=5000

# Reverse proxy. Leave UNSET unless the API genuinely runs behind one you
# control. Setting it makes the API believe X-Forwarded-For, which is what
# request.ip, rate-limit buckets, and IP allowlists key off — trusting it from
# any peer lets a client rotate that header to bypass all three.
#   TRUSTED_PROXIES=1                      # hop count
#   TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/8,172.16.0.1  # IP/CIDR allowlist
# The compose files set this for you: both trust only the panel and portal
# containers, by their fixed addresses on a private network. Never a hop count
# on a network other containers share: any of them could then forge its
# address. The panel and portal forward the visitor's IP, and without trusting
# them every operator and portal visitor shares one bucket. docs/rate-limits.md.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# How the API recognises YOUR reverse proxy: it sends this as the
# X-Rekey-Proxy-Secret request header (a Traefik header middleware in the
# compose files), and only then is X-Forwarded-For believed, taking the
# API_PROXY_HOPS-th entry from the right (1 = Traefik alone, 2 = a CDN in front
# that Traefik trusts). Unset, traffic through an unidentified proxy gets no
# per-IP limits. See docs/rate-limits.md. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# API_PROXY_SECRET=
# API_PROXY_HOPS=1
# Proves a request came from YOUR panel or portal whatever network path it
# took: they send it as X-Rekey-Caller-Secret, and the API then believes the one
# visitor address they send as X-Rekey-Client-Ip. Needed when they call the API's public origin
# (the split Dokploy units); same value on the API, panel and portal.
# INTERNAL_CALLER_SECRET=

# JWT secrets. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# JWT_SECRET: min 32 chars.
JWT_SECRET=

# Optional RS256 private key (PKCS#8/PKCS#1 PEM) for end-user access tokens on
# Applications that set authConfig.tokenAlg = "RS256". When set it is THE active
# signing key; when unset a keypair is generated and stored (encrypted) on first
# use. Literal `\n` sequences are accepted. Public halves served at /.well-known/jwks.json.
# JWT_RS256_PRIVATE_KEY=

# AES-256-GCM key protecting secrets at rest: stored provider credentials
# (Stripe/PayPal/Razorpay), OAuth client secrets, TOTP seeds, SMTP passwords,
# and generated RS256 signing keys. 64 hex characters.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Optional in development. In production the API REFUSES TO BOOT without it —
# booting without it would persist those credentials in plaintext, which is a
# silent exposure rather than a loud one.
#
# It also refuses to boot on a key it knows is public: the value
# docker-compose.yml shipped as a default before 2.0.0-rc.1, or any single
# repeated character. If you were running that default, generate a new key AND
# re-enter the credentials it encrypted — rotating the key alone leaves the
# stored values unreadable — then rotate those credentials at the provider.
#
# Losing this key means losing everything it encrypted. There is no recovery.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Where the panel and SDK should reach this API
API_URL=http://localhost:3030

# Comma-separated allowlist of origins permitted to call the API with cookies.
# Dev permits localhost automatically; in PRODUCTION this MUST be set or every
# cross-origin request is denied (no trailing slashes).
#   CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://panel.example.com,https://admin.example.com
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Public base URL of your customer portal (Portal V2). Its origin is
# auto-allowed for publishable-key requests and folded into CORS.
#
# NO DEFAULT, deliberately: this is the origin your END-USERS get sent to, and
# defaulting it to Rekey's hosted portal would point a self-hoster's customers
# at our infrastructure. Unset means portal links are simply not offered.
# PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL=https://portal.yourdomain.com

# Public base URL of the operator panel. The operator MCP OAuth authorize
# endpoint redirects here (/mcp-consent) so the operator signs in through the
# real panel login (session reuse, passkeys, MFA, magic-link), and emailed
# workspace invites link here.
#
# NO DEFAULT, same reasoning — a Rekey default would send your operators to our
# panel. Unset is fine for local development; set it before you rely on the
# operator MCP or on emailed invites.
# PANEL_URL=https://panel.yourdomain.com
#
# How many reverse proxies stand in front of the PANEL (not the API; that is
# TRUSTED_PROXIES above). The panel forwards one client IP to the API, which
# rate-limits operator sign-in and token refresh on it. 0 (the default) means
# the panel is reached directly: a client-sent X-Forwarded-For is discarded and
# the socket address is used. 1 for one proxy such as Traefik; 2 for a CDN in
# front of Traefik. Setting it higher than the real count lets a client choose
# its own rate-limit bucket.
# PANEL_TRUSTED_PROXIES=0
#
# Required when PANEL_TRUSTED_PROXIES is 1 or more: the proxy sends it as
# X-Rekey-Proxy-Secret (docker-compose.prod.yml wires the Traefik label), and
# the panel believes X-Forwarded-For only from requests that carry it. Without
# it the panel reports the connection address. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# PANEL_PROXY_SECRET=
#
# The panel sends INTERNAL_CALLER_SECRET (when set) to the API as the
# X-Rekey-Caller-Secret header on every server-side call, so the API can believe
# the client IP the panel forwards even through a public origin. The panel and
# the API must hold the same value. Server-only; never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# Panel build-time (NEXT_PUBLIC_*) values. Both are intentionally unset by
# default rather than falling back to Rekey's own infrastructure:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID — unset means the panel loads no analytics.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL        — your hosted-portal origin, if you run one.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTAL_URL=https://portal.example.com

# Public, internet-reachable base URL of THIS API — used to auto-register
# provider webhooks (Stripe/PayPal call back here). Set to your external
# origin in prod, or an ngrok tunnel in dev. Falls back to API_URL; webhook
# auto-config refuses on a localhost base in production.
# PUBLIC_WEBHOOK_BASE_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com

# Bootstrap admin credential — required. Used to mint the first Tenant
# and Application via /api/v1/admin/* before the panel ships, and to reach the
# read-only /api/v1/admin/metrics/* endpoints. Treat it as deployment-wide: it
# is not scoped to a workspace and should never reach a browser.
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
SUPER_ADMIN_KEY=

# Optional network gate on /api/v1/admin/* — comma-separated IPs and/or CIDRs
# (v4 and v6). Unset means no restriction. When set, requests from anywhere
# else are refused with 403 ADMIN_IP_NOT_ALLOWED *before* the key is checked.
# SUPER_ADMIN_KEY is one shared secret covering the whole deployment, so this
# is worth setting if you can: a leaked key alone is then not enough.
# Behind a proxy, set TRUSTED_PROXIES too or every request looks like the proxy.
# A malformed entry fails the boot rather than silently matching nothing.
# ADMIN_IP_ALLOWLIST=203.0.113.4, 10.0.0.0/8

# NOTE: there is deliberately NO deployment-wide Stripe configuration — no API
# key and no STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET. Webhook secrets are per-Application (stored
# with that app's own credentials); a shared one would be a cross-tenant trust
# boundary, and signature verification is offline HMAC needing no account key.
# There is also no stub provider: an Application that has not stored its own
# Stripe credentials refuses checkout with BILLING_CREDENTIALS_NOT_CONFIGURED,
# in dev exactly as in production. Stripe webhooks are per-Application
# only — set the webhook signing secret as a BYO credential on each Application
# (Panel → Billing), and point Stripe at /api/v1/billing/webhook/stripe/<app-slug>.

# Default transactional email (Resend). When set, Applications without BYO email
# credentials send via this shared account. Leave empty for the legacy "Rekey
# returns raw tokens, your app emails them" mode. From-address must be on a
# Resend-verified domain. Generate a key at https://resend.com/api-keys.
# RESEND_DEFAULT_API_KEY=
# RESEND_DEFAULT_FROM=support@yourdomain.com
# RESEND_DEFAULT_FROM_NAME=Rekey

# Deployment-wide fallback for the base URL transactional emails link back to —
# the "Get started" button in the welcome mail, and the base for reset /
# verify / magic-link URLs when your code doesn't pass one.
#
# LAST resort. The order is: the URL your SDK call supplies, then the
# per-Application setting (Panel → Application → Auth → Application URL), then
# the origin of that app's first redirect URL, then this. Set it on a
# single-app self-host to configure the base once instead of per Application.
#
# Unset by default, and leaving it unset is safe: when nothing in the chain
# resolves, emails render WITHOUT their call-to-action button. That is
# deliberate — a button pointing at a placeholder domain is worse than no
# button, and an empty href is the same bug wearing a different hat.
# DEFAULT_APP_URL=https://app.yourdomain.com

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. With no mail transport configured, the operator
# password-reset and magic-link endpoints normally return no token at all, so
# there is no way to complete those flows locally. Set this to see the raw
# token in the API response (the panel then renders a working link).
#
# Never set this on a deployment you care about: those endpoints are
# unauthenticated, so echoing the token lets anyone who knows an operator's
# email take over the workspace. The API refuses to boot if this is set with
# NODE_ENV=production.
# REKEY_DEV_ECHO_AUTH_TOKENS=true

# Rate limiting (all optional, defaults shown). The global limiter keeps one
# bucket per caller: per secret API key, per operator, per signed-in end user,
# else per client IP. Sizing and the reasoning: docs/rate-limits.md.
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX=100                  # unauthenticated requests per window, per client IP
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000          # window length in ms
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTHENTICATED_MAX=600    # per operator / signed-in end user (never below RATE_LIMIT_MAX)
# RATE_LIMIT_API_KEY_MAX=6000         # per secret API key (never below RATE_LIMIT_MAX)
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTHENTICATED_IP_MAX=3000  # per client IP across all its operators / end users (API keys exempt)
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_FAILURE_MAX=100     # rejected credentials (401s) per client IP before it is refused; default RATE_LIMIT_MAX
# RATE_LIMIT_REFRESH_MAX=60           # POST /tenant/auth/refresh, per client IP
# RATE_LIMIT_USAGE_MAX=6000           # POST /usage/record per key; default RATE_LIMIT_API_KEY_MAX

# Operator MCP HTTP server (POST /api/v1/tenant/mcp + its OAuth server).
# Set to false to disable/unmount it entirely (routes 404). Default: enabled.
# Each workspace has its own switch too, and Applications have a separate one
# for the end-user MCP. Which wins, and what each refuses: docs/mcp.md, section
# "Which switch wins".
# OPERATOR_MCP_ENABLED=false

# RFC 7591 dynamic client registration on the OPERATOR MCP authorization server.
#   open     (default) — anyone may register a public client. This is how Claude
#                        Desktop / Claude Code / Cursor connect by discovery.
#   disabled           — POST /oauth/register answers 403, and
#                        registration_endpoint disappears from the RFC 8414
#                        metadata. Clients that already hold a client_id keep
#                        working.
# Worth closing once your clients are connected: registration hands out no data
# and no token (an operator still approves at the panel), but it DOES allowlist
# a redirect_uri of the registrant's choosing — the one ingredient a
# consent-phishing link needs.
# OPERATOR_MCP_DYNAMIC_REGISTRATION=disabled

# Force the `Secure` attribute on session cookies on or off, overriding the
# per-request decision. Leave unset unless you terminate TLS somewhere the web
# apps cannot observe: they read X-Forwarded-Proto, falling back to the Host,
# and mark cookies Secure for anything that is not plain HTTP on loopback.
# Set to `false` ONLY if you serve over plain HTTP on a non-loopback host and
# accept that session cookies travel in the clear.
# REKEY_COOKIE_SECURE=false

# Address docker-compose.yml publishes the api/panel/portal ports on. Loopback
# by default so bringing the local stack up on a VPS doesn't put an
# unauthenticated operator-signup endpoint on the internet. If you set this to
# 0.0.0.0, set OPERATOR_SIGNUP_MODE=invite below in the same edit. Real
# deployments should use docker-compose.prod.yml instead (Traefik, no published
# ports).
# BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# Operator (TenantUser) registration mode. Gates every path that CREATES a new
# operator + workspace; existing operators always sign in.
#   open   (default) — self-serve sign-up open to anyone
#   invite — new operator must redeem a single-use super-admin invite key
#   closed — all new-operator registration disabled
# OPERATOR_SIGNUP_MODE=open

# Whether OWNER/ADMIN may grant a subscription from the panel, with no payment
# provider behind it — an invoiced sale, a comped account, a migration off a
# previous billing system.
#   enabled  (default) — the tenant grant route is live for OWNER/ADMIN
#   disabled — that route 404s; granting stays on the super-admin key
# Granting only. Cancelling is never gated by this — it removes entitlement and
# fails safe.
# Set `disabled` if this deployment SELLS to the workspaces it hosts: there a
# granted subscription can write the granter's own allowance.
# TENANT_SUBSCRIPTION_GRANTS=enabled

# Ceilings stamped onto every workspace this deployment creates — JSON matching
# TenantLimitsSchema. UNSET (the default) means new workspaces get NULL limits,
# which means unlimited: exactly what happens today, so leaving this alone
# changes nothing about your deployment.
#
# Set it when you want a floor under every workspace. Without it, `Tenant.limits`
# is only ever written after the fact via PUT /api/v1/admin/tenants/:id/limits,
# so any workspace nobody runs that against is unbounded — including every one a
# self-serve sign-up produces. Rekey attaches no plan or price to these numbers;
# you supply the policy, the API just applies it. A malformed value fails the
# boot rather than being ignored — a limit you believe is in force but isn't is
# worse than a server that won't start.
# DEFAULT_TENANT_LIMITS={"maxProductionApps":1}

# Whether a signed-in operator may create ADDITIONAL workspaces
# (POST /api/v1/tenant/workspace). open (default) | disabled.
#
# Default is today's behavior. Set `disabled` on a deployment that decides which
# workspaces exist: a ceiling per workspace means nothing if any operator —
# including someone merely invited into a team — can mint a fresh workspace with
# a fresh ceiling. Gates CREATION only; switching between workspaces, listing,
# renaming and leaving all keep working, so operators already in several are
# unaffected.
# WORKSPACE_CREATION=open

# Panel-side WebAuthn (operator passkeys) relying-party config. When RP_ID is
# unset, operator passkey endpoints refuse with WEBAUTHN_NOT_CONFIGURED. RP_ID is
# the panel hostname (no scheme/port); RP_ORIGINS is a comma-separated origin
# allowlist; RP_NAME is the display name.
# PANEL_WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=panel.yourdomain.com
# PANEL_WEBAUTHN_RP_ORIGINS=https://panel.yourdomain.com
# PANEL_WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Rekey

# Panel-side operator social login (for the PANEL, not end-users). A provider
# activates only when BOTH its id and secret are set. Redirect base defaults to
# the first (panel.) CORS origin.
# PANEL_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# PANEL_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PANEL_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# PANEL_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PANEL_OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE=https://panel.yourdomain.com

# "Sign in with <this deployment>" on the panel: operators sign in with an
# account on one of this deployment's OWN Applications. All three together or
# the button stays hidden. The issuer is an Application with OIDC enabled, and
# it must have requireEmailVerification on so the `email` scope is offered.
# PANEL_OAUTH_REKEY_CLIENT_ID=
# PANEL_OAUTH_REKEY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PANEL_OAUTH_REKEY_ISSUER=https://api.yourdomain.com/api/v1/mcp/<slug>

# Operator sign-in by ID Token assertion (POST /api/v1/tenant/auth/oidc/assert).
# Trusts ID Tokens this deployment itself issued for ISSUER, with audience
# CLIENT_ID. Both must be set or the endpoint 404s. See
# docs/operator-oidc-assertion.md.
# OPERATOR_OIDC_ISSUER=https://api.yourdomain.com/api/v1/mcp/<slug>
# OPERATOR_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=

# Global kill-switch for the HIBP Pwned Passwords breach check. When `true`, all
# per-Application passwordBreachCheckEnabled flags are ignored (offline/restricted-egress).
# HIBP_BREACH_CHECK_DISABLED=false

# Escape hatch to allow outbound webhooks to private/localhost targets. Leave
# `false` on public deploys to prevent SSRF via attacker-controlled endpoint URLs.
# WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_TARGETS=false


# Retention is opt-in. Unset (or 0), nothing below is ever pruned and every
# row is kept forever. Set a positive number of days to turn a sweep on.

# How long a processed inbound billing webhook is remembered for replay
# suppression. Rows older than this are pruned; a provider that redelivers an
# event after the window would be applied twice, so keep it well above the
# longest retry schedule your providers use. 90 is a sensible window.
# WEBHOOK_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# How long the append-only log tables keep rows in Postgres: security_events,
# email_logs, and finished webhook_deliveries (pending deliveries are never
# pruned). usage_records is a billing ledger and is not covered. Swept every
# 10 minutes in bounded batches. 30 matches the window hosted identity
# providers offer.
# LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Optional: copy rows to S3-compatible storage BEFORE they are pruned, as
# gzipped NDJSON under <prefix><table>/dt=YYYY-MM-DD/. A failed upload leaves
# the rows in place for the next sweep. Set endpoint, bucket and both keys
# together or not at all: a partial set refuses to boot.
#
# Cloudflare R2:  endpoint https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com, region auto
# AWS S3:         endpoint https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com, region <region>
#
# Archived rows are NOT reached by end-user erasure. They carry IP addresses,
# user agents and recipient email addresses; give the bucket a lifecycle rule
# that matches your retention policy. See docs/data-erasure.md.
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_ENDPOINT=
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_BUCKET=
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_REGION=auto
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# LOG_ARCHIVE_S3_PREFIX=rekey/

# Session lifetimes. Access tokens are short-lived and renewed with a refresh
# token; refresh tokens are rotated on every use and sliding, so a client that
# checks in at least once per window stays signed in indefinitely. Size the
# refresh window to the longest gap between uses (a desktop app that opens
# monthly wants 60 or 90 days); keep the access token short unless the machine
# is trusted, since a revoked membership keeps acting until its next renewal.
# Defaults: 15 minutes and 30 days for both kinds.
# END_USER_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=900        # 60 .. 86400
# END_USER_REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=30           # 1 .. 365
# OPERATOR_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=900        # 60 .. 43200 (12 hours)
# OPERATOR_REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL_DAYS=30           # 1 .. 365
